\section{A Borrowed Name, Not a Borrowed Language}

The Vienna Architecture Description Language (VADL) is a processor description language developed at TU Wien. Our reference is Himmelbauer and colleagues' February 2024 preprint, arXiv:2402.09087v1 \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}. Its introduction explicitly credits the \emph{name} to the Vienna Definition Language (VDL), developed to give PL/I an operational semantics. VDL made the behavior of a programming language precise through an abstract machine. VADL makes the externally visible behavior and binary representation of a processor precise, then uses that description to generate parts of its toolchain. The family resemblance is the use of explicit operations on abstract state; the paper does not claim that modern VADL is a successor dialect of VDL.

The Vienna Development Method (VDM) is another name from the broader Vienna formal-specification tradition. It is a method and family of notations for specifying and developing systems, not an alternative expansion of VDL or VADL. Neither VDL's use for PL/I nor VDM's use for system models implies any shared syntax, component, or software implementation with VADL. Here ``architecture description'' means a model of a programmable processor, not a notation for decomposing arbitrary software into components.

\section{Describe the Interface Before the Pipeline}

In Sections~3.1--3.2 the paper separates the instruction set architecture (ISA), which defines observable execution and encodings, from the microarchitecture (MiA), which describes how a processor implements that contract. An ISA does not refer to a MiA. A multi-stage implementation may split fetch, decode, execution, and retirement; a functional simulator does not need those boundaries. By contrast, a cycle-accurate simulator or hardware generator requires enough structural detail to give those boundaries meaning.

These are selected top-level lines from Listing~1, with spacing normalized. Empty bodies illustrate references between descriptions; they are not enough to build a processor.

\begin{verbatim}
import rv32i::RV32I
instruction set architecture RV32IM extending RV32I = { }
application binary interface ABI for RV32IM = { }
micro processor CPU implements RV32IM with ABI = { }
micro architecture FiveStage implements CPU = { }
\end{verbatim}

An ISA extension adds or changes architectural definitions within the language's extension mechanism; it is not another pipeline for the same instructions. An application binary interface (ABI) assigns software roles such as stack pointer or argument registers to ISA resources. That information is not recoverable simply by observing that the architecture has a register file and a jump instruction. A concrete pipeline, in turn, may provide optional scheduling knowledge to a compiler, but the operation that an instruction performs does not depend on the schedule. Section~4.1 puts these choices behind a shared intermediate representation, VIR: the front end resolves names and types after macro expansion, and consumers work on the analyzed representation rather than parse the source independently. \Cref{fig:visual-323} shows the basic dependencies, excluding optional scheduling data.

