\section{Make One Change and Watch the Right Outputs}

Suppose an architect widens a toy instruction's immediate without changing word length. Later, an implementation engineer adds forwarding without changing the instruction at all. A useful review plan for the two changes is:

\begin{verbatim}
edit A: widen signed immediate from 5 to 6 bits
  recheck encodings         # where does the extra bit come from?
  regenerate ISA consumers # compiler, assembler, decoder, ISS
  test +16                 # now selectable; formerly out of range

edit B: forward a result to the next dependent operation
  keep the exact same program bytes
  compare architectural retirement traces  # expect agreement
  compare cycles for a dependent pair      # a stall may disappear
\end{verbatim}

The first decision requires a check that the extra bit does not overlap another field or turn yesterday's invalid words into unintended aliases. Avoid trusting a closed loop between the new assembler and the new simulator: they could share the same misunderstanding. Include fixed bytes from an independent encoding calculation. The second decision leaves the architected register trace alone; its test must count cycles or inspect timing, otherwise a forwarding change that simply drops a required stall might evade a test that exercises only independent instructions.

\begin{verbatim}
# Reference-level check for one dependent pair
retire(ADD x3, x1, x2); retire(ADD x4, x3, x5)
assert architectural_registers == reference_registers

# MiA-level check: generated hazard logic may change this only
assert cycles_with_forwarding <= cycles_without_forwarding
\end{verbatim}

The first assertion is an ISA contract: the second ADD must observe the value ultimately written by the first. The second is a performance expectation for this particular dependency, not an ISA rule. A MiA generator may implement forwarding with a bypass network and adjust generated hazard detection accordingly; it must not make an architectural consumer silently observe an earlier value. This separation also makes optimization review concrete: dead-code elimination on VIR may remove unused pure computations, whereas a forwarding optimization belongs to the synthesized timing implementation and needs a timing-aware test.

\section{Read the Evidence at the Level It Was Collected}

Reducing hand-maintained duplication across artifacts is a design goal; transparent retargeting of every artifact is not established by that goal. Section~5.1 compares raw and macro-expanded specifications, demonstrating concision on selected descriptions. Section~5.4 reports a prototype LLVM backend that completed only four of the 22 Embench programs; the test was at unoptimized settings. Sections~5.6--5.8 evaluate simulator and hardware artifacts under their own conditions. These are different measurements, not a general certification or a claim about today's tools. Treat the description as a common place to make and audit changes, and each generated artifact as a separate place to verify their consequences.
